"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,367\nIllusions of learning\n\n4\n00:00:12,167 --> 00:00:14,234\nRereading your notes feels productive.\n\n5\n00:00:14,643 --> 00:00:16,066\nThe words look familiar,\n\n6\n00:00:16,086 --> 00:00:18,151\nand familiar feels like learned.\n\n7\n00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:20,267\nBut that feeling can fool you.\n\n8\n00:00:20,437 --> 00:00:23,985\nIn a survey of a hundred and seventy seven college students,\n\n9\n00:00:24,330 --> 00:00:27,821\neighty four percent said they reread notes or textbooks,\n\n10\n00:00:28,076 --> 00:00:31,112\nand fifty five percent called it their top strategy.\n\n11\n00:00:31,665 --> 00:00:34,701\nOnly eleven percent said they tested themselves,\n\n12\n00:00:34,955 --> 00:00:37,412\nand just one percent ranked that first.\n\n13\n00:00:37,582 --> 00:00:39,342\nRereading builds fluency.\n\n14\n00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:42,996\nThe material feels easy because you have just seen it.\n\n15\n00:00:43,443 --> 00:00:48,350\nBut recognizing something is not the same as being able to recall it in an exam.\n\n16\n00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,347\nIn one classic experiment,\n\n17\n00:00:50,585 --> 00:00:54,723\nstudents who reread a passage did slightly better five minutes later.\n\n18\n00:00:55,277 --> 00:00:56,319\nBut after a week,\n\n19\n00:00:56,609 --> 00:01:00,662\nstudents who had practiced recalling it remembered fifty six percent,\n\n20\n00:01:00,969 --> 00:01:03,882\ncompared with forty two percent for the rereaders.\n\n21\n00:01:04,052 --> 00:01:06,139\nSpacing shows the same illusion.\n\n22\n00:01:06,563 --> 00:01:10,038\nStudents learned painters' styles with each artist in a block,\n\n23\n00:01:10,273 --> 00:01:12,010\nor with artists mixed together.\n\n24\n00:01:12,525 --> 00:01:14,522\nMixing worked better for most of them.\n\n25\n00:01:14,876 --> 00:01:18,348\nYet seventy eight percent believed blocking had helped more,\n\n26\n00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:21,213\neven after their own test showed the opposite.\n\n27\n00:01:21,736 --> 00:01:27,836\nA large online replication in 2014 found a similar advantage for mixing.\n\n28\n00:01:28,006 --> 00:01:28,987\nSome caution.\n\n29\n00:01:29,354 --> 00:01:32,655\nThese are mostly lab tasks with short materials.\n\n30\n00:01:32,898 --> 00:01:37,801\nAnd rereading is not useless: a first read is how you take the material in.\n\n31\n00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:41,237\nThe problem is relying on it to check what you know.\n\n32\n00:01:41,407 --> 00:01:42,273\nTry this.\n\n33\n00:01:42,643 --> 00:01:46,060\nAfter reading, close the book and write down what you remember.\n\n34\n00:01:46,385 --> 00:01:47,947\nQuiz yourself with questions,\n\n35\n00:01:47,967 --> 00:01:48,933\nnot highlights.\n\n36\n00:01:49,215 --> 00:01:52,204\nMix topics, and space sessions across days.\n\n37\n00:01:52,476 --> 00:01:54,086\nAnd when something feels easy,\n\n38\n00:01:54,106 --> 00:01:55,810\ntest it before you trust it.\n\n39\n00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,246\nFeeling fluent is not the same as knowing.\n\n40\n00:01:58,430 --> 00:01:59,147\nTest it.\n\n"
